
[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a [1mbounded[0m task — the login flow already exists in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (one function, one caller), so I'll present a short
  design in chat rather than write a spec. But there's a real fork I can't resolve from the request.

  [1mThe problem:[0m [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m is called from the form submit handler at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m, and at that moment nothing
  in the app knows a user ID. The form collects only username and password ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m). A [38;5;153muserId[39m [3mparameter[0m has to
  come from somewhere, and the three plausible sources give you three different features.

  [1mThe fork[0m — what "track who logged in" means, and where the ID originates:

  - [1mReturn the ID instead of passing it in.[0m The server identifies the user; [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m and
    the handler logs it. Buys you the actual tracking you asked for with no caller changes and no made-up data. Costs:
    not a parameter, so if some other caller is meant to supply the ID this doesn't serve it. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAdd [38;5;153muserId[39m as a third parameter.[0m [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. Buys a caller-supplied identity — useful if an
    ID exists upstream (a tenant, an account picked on a prior screen). Costs: the only existing caller has nothing real
    to pass, so it'd pass [38;5;153mnull[39m or a placeholder until something upstream provides one. This one changes the signature,
    so every future caller inherits it — the expensive-to-undo option.
  - [1mPass a client-generated correlation ID.[0m [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, requestId)[39m with a UUID minted at submit. Buys
    request tracing across attempts including failed ones. Costs: it tracks the [3mattempt[0m, not the person — it won't
    answer "who logged in".

  [1mMy recommendation: return the ID.[0m "Track who logged in" is a fact the login result establishes, not an input the
  caller already holds. Passing in an ID the caller doesn't have means inventing a value to satisfy the signature. If
  you do have an upstream ID source I haven't seen, the second option becomes right and I'll take it.
